---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import SpecTable from '../../components/SpecTable.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/paths';

const pageTitle = 'Telegram Automation Security & Isolation';
const pageDesc =
  'How the platform keeps tenants apart: fail-closed identity scoping across bot, dashboard, mobile, REST and gRPC, AES-256-GCM at rest — and its stated limits.';

const toc = [
  { id: 'deep-dives', title: 'Security deep dives' },
  { id: 'model', title: 'Isolation model' },
  { id: 'enforcement', title: 'Where it is enforced' },
  { id: 'crypto', title: 'Cryptography' },
  { id: 'secrets', title: 'Secrets handling' },
  { id: 'network', title: 'Network posture' },
  { id: 'audit', title: 'Audit trail' },
  { id: 'limits', title: 'Known limits' },
];

const layers = [
  {
    name: 'Telegram bot',
    detail: 'Commands resolve the caller to a numeric user id and load only that account\'s tasks, sessions and history.',
    icon: 'bot',
  },
  {
    name: 'Web dashboard',
    detail: 'Bearer token from the authentication routes; every request carries the resolved identity into the handler.',
    icon: 'devices',
  },
  {
    name: 'Mobile app',
    detail: 'Signs in through the same routes and talks to the same API — there is no second, weaker path.',
    icon: 'phone',
  },
  {
    name: 'REST API',
    detail: 'Tenant-scoped handlers; an unlinked account resolves to id 0 and therefore owns nothing.',
    icon: 'chip',
  },
  {
    name: 'gRPC services',
    detail: 'Internal calls carry the caller identity that the control plane verified, never one the client asserts.',
    icon: 'server',
  },
  {
    name: 'Administration',
    detail: 'Mounted behind RequireAdmin: without admin rights the route set is unreachable, not merely hidden.',
    icon: 'shield',
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDesc}
  path="/security/"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: url('/') },
    { name: 'Reference', href: url('/api/') },
    { name: 'Security', href: url('/security/') },
  ]}
  toc={toc}
  keywords={['Telegram forwarding security', 'multi-tenant isolation', 'fail-closed design']}
>
  <p>
    AutoTelegramForward runs one installation for one operator's accounts — and optionally for many. The design decision
    that shapes everything else is that isolation is <strong>fail-closed</strong>: the default answer to "may this caller
    read this row?" is no, and a caller who has not linked a Telegram account can read nothing at all.
  </p>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="deep-dives">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Security deep dives</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      This page states the model. Three companions show the machinery underneath it — which query carries the owner,
      where a secret can be read back, and what a hostile request meets before it reaches a handler.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-3.5 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {
        [
          { title: 'Tenant isolation', href: url('/security/tenant-isolation/'), desc: 'Owner scoping per surface, the three refusals, the owner-zero trap and the tests that enforce it.', icon: 'users' },
          { title: 'Secrets & keys', href: url('/security/secrets/'), desc: 'What is encrypted, what is write-only, what redaction does to a phone number, and how to rotate each credential.', icon: 'key' },
          { title: 'Request hardening', href: url('/security/request-hardening/'), desc: 'Body caps, server timeouts, the endpoint guard that blocks SSRF, and the shutdown drain.', icon: 'shield' },
        ].map((card) => (
          <a class="card no-underline block" href={card.href}>
            <span class="inline-flex w-9 h-9 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-900/60 text-sky-300 mb-3">
              <Icon name={card.icon} class="w-4 h-4" />
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1.5">{card.title}</h3>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">{card.desc}</p>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="model">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Isolation model</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Identity is a number, not a claim. Each surface resolves the caller to a numeric Telegram user id, that id travels
      with the request through every layer, and each data access is scoped by it.
    </p>

    <div class="grid gap-3.5 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {
        layers.map((layer) => (
          <div class="card">
            <span class="inline-flex w-9 h-9 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-900/60 text-sky-300 mb-3">
              <Icon name={layer.icon} class="w-4 h-4" />
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1.5">{layer.name}</h3>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">{layer.detail}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="enforcement">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Where it is enforced</h2>
    <SpecTable
      headers={['Control', 'Implementation', 'Failure mode']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>Ownership checks</strong>', 'Every task, session, filter, AI configuration and prompt template is read through the caller\'s own scope.', 'Not-yours and not-found return the same 404, so ids cannot be probed.'],
        ['<strong>Unlinked accounts</strong>', 'A zero user id is treated as owning nothing.', 'Empty collections, never a fallback to a shared account.'],
        ['<strong>Admin routes</strong>', 'Mounted behind an admin requirement in the parent mux', 'The route does not exist for non-admins.'],
        ['<strong>Simulation</strong>', 'The dry-run endpoint loads the rule through the same ownership check', 'You can only preview rules you own.'],
        ['<strong>Log history</strong>', 'Queries are filtered server-side by actor and scope before returning rows', 'No client-side filtering to bypass.'],
        ['<strong>Backups</strong>', 'Export and import are tenant-scoped and encrypted', 'A stolen file is useless without the passphrase.'],
      ]}
      caption="Isolation controls and what happens when they say no"
    />

    <Callout type="success" class="mt-4" title="Tested, not asserted">
      <p>
        Tenant isolation has dedicated Go test files in the API package and Python tests in the core. That is why the
        front page can claim zero cross-tenant leaks without an asterisk.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="crypto">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Cryptography</h2>
    <SpecTable
      headers={['Data', 'Protection', 'Notes']}
      rows={[
        ['Session strings and API keys at rest', '<strong>AES-256-GCM</strong>', 'Authenticated encryption; a modified ciphertext fails to decrypt rather than decrypting wrongly.'],
        ['Account passwords', '<strong>PBKDF2-SHA256</strong>, 240 000 iterations, per-user salt', 'Verified in constant time; the iteration count is stored per row so it can be raised later.'],
        ['Configuration backups', '<strong>AES-256-GCM bundle</strong>', 'Portable between servers: export on one host, import on the next.'],
        ['Session backups', '<strong>Encrypted archive</strong>', 'Written only after the export succeeds, and never in plaintext.'],
        ['Web traffic', '<strong>TLS</strong>', 'Terminate at your reverse proxy; the app itself is meant to sit behind one.'],
      ]}
      caption="Cryptographic primitives in use"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="secrets">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Secrets handling</h2>
    <ul class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0 mb-4">
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span><strong class="text-slate-100">The API never echoes a secret.</strong> The environment-contract route reports <code>set: true/false</code> per key plus a fix hint — never the value.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span><strong class="text-slate-100">Validation scans for committed secrets</strong> as part of the read-only check set, alongside layout, compose, catalog parity and ports.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span><strong class="text-slate-100">Backups can be exported without secrets</strong>, so a configuration bundle can be shared for review while credentials stay home.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span><strong class="text-slate-100">Setup writes <code>.env</code> with restrictive permissions</strong> and refuses to continue when a required key is missing or malformed.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <a href={url('/getting-started/configuration/')} class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">
      Environment key contract
      <Icon name="arrow-right" class="w-3.5 h-3.5" />
    </a>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="network">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Network posture</h2>
    <SpecTable
      headers={['Surface', 'Default exposure', 'Hardening']}
      rows={[
        ['Dashboard/API', 'Behind your reverse proxy', 'TLS at the proxy; the app trusts only what the proxy forwards.'],
        ['Bot', 'Outbound to Telegram only', 'No inbound listener beyond the Telegram connection itself.'],
        ['gRPC core', 'Loopback inside the compose network', 'Containers reach each other by service name; nothing is published.'],
        ['Logger service', 'Loopback inside the compose network', 'The API proxies log queries, so clients never address it directly.'],
        ['PostgreSQL', 'Loopback unless you expose it', 'Use the bundled default for single-host installs.'],
        ['Health endpoints', 'Public', 'Return booleans and latencies only — never configuration values.'],
      ]}
      caption="What listens where, and how to tighten it"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="audit">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Audit trail</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      The access logger mirrors every request — successes included, not just failures — into the central logger with the
      verified actor, the subject task and the correlation id. That is what makes "who changed this rule, and what did
      it do next" answerable after the fact.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-3.5 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1.5">Attributable entries</h3>
        <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-slate-400">Each entry names the actor, the service, the level and the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1.5">Bounded queries</h3>
        <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-slate-400">Filter by service, level, text and time; results page instead of flooding.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1.5">Exportable evidence</h3>
        <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-slate-400">History exports as JSON or CSV, and purge is an explicit, scoped action.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="limits">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Known limits</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Being straight about the boundaries is part of the design. These are the things you should know before you rely on
      the platform in a regulated setting.
    </p>
    <ul class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="info" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-amber-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>Static analysis reports pre-existing findings in the backup export helper. They are known, tracked and do not grant a tenant access to another tenant's data — but the export path deserves your own review before you handle third-party data.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="info" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-amber-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>TLS is expected at your reverse proxy. The service itself speaks plain HTTP on the internal network by design.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="info" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-amber-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>Encryption keys are your responsibility: lose the key material and encrypted backups cannot be recovered, by us or by anyone.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="info" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-amber-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>Your Telegram accounts remain subject to Telegram's own rate limits and terms; the platform is built to respect FloodWait rather than defeat it.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
